# Rust build artefacts
/target/
**/*.rs.bk
*.pdb

# Cargo.lock IS committed. This workspace ships binaries (server, client, bot),
# and reproducible builds of a binary need a pinned dependency graph — doubly so
# for a project whose CI gate is a cross-platform determinism hash.

# Fuzzing (cargo-fuzz, from Task 06 onward).
#
# The CORPUS IS COMMITTED — it is the seed set of real encoded messages, and a
# fuzzer starting from valid framing finds far more than one starting from
# noise. Build output and crash artefacts are not.
/fuzz/target/
/fuzz/artifacts/

# The glTF corpus is SEEDS AND REGRESSIONS ONLY, which departs from the rule
# above deliberately. A protocol seed is tens of bytes; a `.glb` is tens of
# kilobytes, and ten minutes of fuzzing left a 12 MB corpus. The named `.glb`
# files are the seed set (`cargo run -p tiamot-core --example gltf_seeds`) plus
# every input that has ever crashed the reader; everything a fuzzer derives from
# those is reproducible in minutes and is not worth carrying in the history.
/fuzz/corpus/gltf_ingest/*
!/fuzz/corpus/gltf_ingest/*.glb

# The audio corpus, on the same rule and for the same reason: a WAV seed is a
# few kilobytes and a fuzzer derives megabytes from it in minutes. The named
# files are the seed set (`cargo run -p client --example write_reference_sounds`)
# plus anything that has ever crashed the decoder. `.ogg` is listed as well as
# `.wav` because a real Vorbis file is the seed this most wants and cannot
# generate — `tone.ogg` is committed for exactly that reason. See
# `fuzz/fuzz_targets/ogg_ingest.rs`.
/fuzz/corpus/ogg_ingest/*
!/fuzz/corpus/ogg_ingest/*.wav
!/fuzz/corpus/ogg_ingest/*.ogg
# The dialog corpus, on the same rule as the two above. A seed is a few dozen
# bytes and five minutes of fuzzing left 1,131 derived entries — which went into
# a commit once before this rule existed. The named files are the seed set
# (`cargo run -p tiamot-core --example dialog_seeds`) plus anything that has
# ever crashed the target; everything a fuzzer derives from those is
# reproducible in minutes and is not worth carrying in the history.
/fuzz/corpus/dialog_schema/*
!/fuzz/corpus/dialog_schema/seed-*.bin
!/fuzz/corpus/dialog_schema/regression-*.bin

/fuzz/coverage/
/fuzz/Cargo.lock

# Criterion benchmark output
/target/criterion/

# Local server state
/server.toml
/world/
*.sqlite
*.sqlite-wal
*.sqlite-shm

# Editors and OS
.idea/
*.swp
*.swo
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# VS Code: keep shared settings, ignore per-developer ones
.vscode/*
!.vscode/settings.json
!.vscode/extensions.json
!.vscode/launch.json

# Task 02b spike artefacts. `measurements.txt` IS committed — the verdict memo
# cites it as the record of what was measured. The OBJ files are regenerated in
# seconds by `cargo run -p subnode-spike --release -- collision` and one of them
# is nearly a megabyte, so they stay out of history.
/spikes/subnode/out/*.obj

# Session traces. `TIAMOT_TRACE_FRAMES`, `TIAMOT_TRACE_PHYSICS` and
# `TIAMOT_TRACE_SERVER` write these, and a run of them is tens of thousands of
# lines about one machine on one afternoon. The findings belong in a commit
# message; the logs do not belong in history. The chunk-frame bug (d5ed968) was
# read out of a set that lived here.
/Frames-Physics/
